Adobe used its MAX conference in Miami Beach to announce a long list of AI changes on 10 November 2026, centered on the idea of an AI agent that works alongside creators. This post reads two Adobe newsroom releases and sorts the announcements by what Adobe says is usable today, what is still in beta or preview, and what the releases do not say.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Adobe's release mentions connectors for Anthropic and OpenAI, so treat the author as having an interest in that part. Stackbrook did not test Adobe Firefly or any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Adobe announced

The Firefly release is dated 10 November 2026 and describes Firefly as an AI creative studio. Its centerpiece is the Adobe Creative Agent, which powers the Firefly AI Assistant, labelled beta. Adobe lists new controls for it: customization, so you can set formats, colors, brand guidelines, characters or products once; a Plan Mode, where you review and approve the agent's plan before it starts or let it run; and a Model Selector, where you pick a model for a session or leave it on automatic choice.

Adobe also describes always-on agents, labelled preview, that keep working in the background on recurring jobs such as organizing assets, checking quality and preparing content for different channels. Alongside the agent come several workspaces. Firefly Video gets expanded editing, including a simplified timeline, captions, and generated transitions and b-roll. Firefly Design, labelled beta, is a single canvas for illustration, image editing, animation, 3D and print. Firefly Graph, also beta, lets you build repeatable workflows that combine AI models and Adobe tools. Firefly Builder turns plain-language ideas into interactive prototypes such as a website, presentation or app, and is in private beta with a waitlist.

On the image side, Adobe names precision tools called Edit Object, Edit Text, Relight and Retouch, plus Quick Actions, a gallery of ready-made workflows such as Remove Object, Thumbnail Generator and Build Product Ad.

## What is available today and what is not

Adobe's own availability section is short, and it is the most useful part of the release for anyone deciding whether to try something. It says Edit Text, Retouch, Relight and Quick Actions are generally available. It says other new tools and capabilities are in beta, and Firefly Builder is private beta through a waitlist. The chart below counts only the items that carry an explicit label in the release.

![Column chart of Firefly items by release status as labelled by Adobe](/images/site1/adobe-firefly-max-2026-agents-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

There is one wrinkle worth knowing. The Firefly release names Edit Object among the image tools but does not list it in the generally available group, so we do not claim it is live. Likewise, Firefly Video, Plan Mode, the Model Selector and the other new items have no individual label in the release beyond the general statement that the other new tools are in beta. Adobe gives no dates for any move from beta or preview to general availability.

## Models and partners

Adobe says Firefly brings together its own models and models from partners, naming Topaz Labs, ByteDance, Google, Kling, OpenAI and ElevenLabs, plus others. The Firefly release lists recent additions as Dreamina Seedance 2.5, Kling 4.0, GPT Image 2.6 Sunburst, GPT Image 2.6 Flare and Nano Banana 2.1. For a small business, the practical meaning is that one subscription surface may give access to several vendors' models, but the release does not say which plans include which models or what each generation costs.

## The Creative Cloud side

A second release covers the wider product line. Adobe says the Creative Agent powers expanded AI Assistants, in beta, in Illustrator, Premiere, InDesign, After Effects and Frame.io. It also describes local MCP integrations, in preview, for Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere and After Effects. In plain terms, this lets you direct a third-party AI agent of your choice to edit native project files on your own machine, with layers, sequences and compositions staying editable. The page does not name which third-party agents are supported or explain setup.

Other items include Generative Restyle and Relight in Photoshop, a Color Mode for color grading in Premiere, Open Eyes in Lightroom (beta) and co-editing in InDesign (beta). For larger organizations the release adds GenStudio tools such as an enterprise coworker interface for campaign work and a brand-standards agent. Adobe also announced a multi-year alliance with the Walt Disney Company, expanded work with Google including connectors for Gemini, and optimization for Arm-based Windows PCs. None of those come with dates in the page we read.

## What the releases do not say

Neither release states a price, a credit allowance, a usage limit or which subscription plan includes any feature. They point readers to Firefly's pricing page, which we did not fetch for this post, so any figure you see elsewhere should be checked against Adobe directly. The releases also give no regions, no device requirements beyond the Arm Windows note, and no independent measurements of how well the agents perform. Adobe cites one survey figure, saying 78 percent of creators report that creative AI has accelerated the growth of their business or following, from its own Creators' Toolkit Report; we did not read that report.

## A cautious way to approach it

This is general information, not legal or financial advice. If you run a small business and make marketing images or short videos, the sensible reading of the availability section is to try the generally available tools first, treat beta features as subject to change, and avoid building a recurring workflow on a preview feature until Adobe commits to terms. If you use a tool that connects an outside agent to your project files, review what access you are granting before you switch it on. Stackbrook has not tested any of this, so every capability above is Adobe's description, not a verified result.
